% ==========================================
% ABSTRACT
% ==========================================
\begin{abstract}
As academic institutions increasingly adopt AI-driven engagement analytics, a critical ``Silo Problem'' emerges: engagement recognition models trained at one university suffer 8-24\% accuracy degradation when deployed at other institutions due to domain shift in lighting conditions, architectural layouts, and demographic distributions. While federated learning enables collaborative training without raw data sharing, existing approaches assume homogeneous edge devices within a single administrative domain. We present \textbf{Hierarchical Federated Averaging (H-FedAvg)}, a three-tier architecture (Classroom $\rightarrow$ Campus $\rightarrow$ Global Federation) designed for cross-institutional collaboration under heterogeneous connectivity and institutional governance constraints. Our staleness-aware asynchronous protocol dampens delayed campus updates while maintaining convergence, and hierarchical differential privacy preserves student identities across aggregation layers. Evaluation across 5 simulated campuses (250 edge nodes, 28,951 samples) demonstrates a 4.8\% absolute cross-domain generalization improvement (88.5\% $\rightarrow$ 93.3\%) while reducing bandwidth costs on the order of $10^4$--$10^5\times$ versus centralized training. The system tolerates 40\% campus dropout with <2\% accuracy loss, providing a production-viable framework for academic AI consortiums designed to operationally align with GDPR/DPDP data-minimization principles.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Federated Learning, Hierarchical Aggregation, Cross-Domain Generalization, Differential Privacy, Non-IID Data, Smart Campus, Institutional Governance.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% ==========================================
% I. INTRODUCTION
% ==========================================
\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation: The Academic Silo Problem}

The deployment of AI-driven student engagement systems across multiple universities faces a fundamental \textbf{domain shift} challenge. An engagement recognition model achieving 96.7\% accuracy at University A (modern lecture halls, LED lighting, homogeneous demographics) degrades to 84.7\% when deployed at University B (heritage buildings, tungsten lighting, diverse populations). This performance collapse occurs because:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Environmental Heterogeneity}: Lighting spectra vary 2.1$\times$ (400-850nm), architectural occlusion rates span 10-65\%, and camera mounting heights differ by 1.5m across institutions.
    \item \textbf{Demographic Non-IID}: Student populations exhibit distinct ethnic, age, and attire distributions. For instance, a model trained on a dataset with 80\% Caucasian and 10\% Asian faces may perform poorly in a campus where the demographic is 60\% Asian and 30\% Hispanic. Statistical analysis shows abstract feature distribution shifts of 0.5-1.2 standard deviations in embedding space between campuses due to these variances.
    \item \textbf{Institutional Isolation}: Universities cannot centralize training data due to GDPR Article 5(1)(c) data minimization and DPDP Act 2023 jurisdictional custody requirements.
\end{itemize}



Existing solutions fail at scale: (1) Per-campus training yields siloed models with poor generalization; (2) Centralization violates privacy regulations and incurs prohibitive bandwidth costs (\$600K/year for 50-campus deployment); (3) Single-server federated learning assumes homogeneous clients and ignores institutional governance boundaries.

\subsection{Research Question}

\textit{Can multiple universities collaboratively train robust AI models without sharing raw student data, while respecting institutional autonomy and tolerating heterogeneous network connectivity?}

\subsection{Our Approach: Hierarchical FedAvg}

We propose a \textbf{three-tier federated architecture} mirroring academic organizational structure:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Tier 1 (Edge):} Classroom nodes perform local SGD on private data, emit only gradient updates.
    \item \textbf{Tier 2 (Campus):} Institutional aggregators combine classroom gradients behind administrative firewalls, add hierarchical DP noise.
    \item \textbf{Tier 3 (Global):} Federation coordinator aggregates campus updates with staleness-aware weighting, broadcasts global model.
\end{enumerate}

This topology enables: (1) \textbf{Privacy} through two-layer aggregation (no raw data leaves classrooms, no classroom-level data leaves campuses); (2) \textbf{Governance} via campus-controlled participation (institutions retain right to withdraw); (3) \textbf{Resilience} through asynchronous updates (slow campuses do not block fast campuses).

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Cross-Institutional Federated Intelligence Architecture}: We propose a three-tier topology that maps federated aggregation steps to physical governance boundaries, ensuring institutional data sovereignty.
    \item \textbf{Staleness-Aware H-FedAvg Protocol}: A novel aggregation mechanism utilizing a polynomial dampening factor to integrate delayed updates from resource-constrained campuses without destabilizing convergence.
    \item \textbf{Hierarchical Privacy Composition}: A two-tier differential privacy framework (edge-level local DP + campus-level central DP) using linear composition to bound cumulative privacy loss across aggregation layers.
    \item \textbf{Systemic Resilience Validation}: Empirical stress testing across 5 simulated campuses (250 nodes, 28K samples) validating 40\% dropout tolerance and 27,000$\times$ bandwidth reduction compared to centralized baselines.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Scope and Independence}
This paper addresses \textbf{cross-institutional federated learning} with institutional governance constraints—a distinct problem from intra-campus federated learning or edge deployment infrastructure. The protocol can be validated independently using any federated learning testbed with simulated campus boundaries, ensuring that the algorithmic contributions stand apart from specific hardware implementations.

\subsection{Reproducibility and Integration with ScholarMaster Engine}
The hierarchical federated learning protocol presented here provides a cross-institutional collaboration layer for edge AI systems, with reference implementations available in the ScholarMaster Engine repository \cite{scholarmaster_repo}. It integrates with edge MLOps pipelines to enable cross-site model synchronization.

% ==========================================
% II. RELATED WORK
% ==========================================
\section{Related Work}

\subsection{Federated Learning Foundations}

McMahan et al. introduced Federated Averaging (FedAvg) for mobile keyboard prediction, establishing the paradigm of client-side training with server-side aggregation. However, FedAvg assumes: (1) clients within a single administrative domain, (2) synchronous communication, (3) IID or mildly non-IID data. Our work relaxes all three assumptions.

\subsection{Hierarchical & Edge FL}
To address network latency, Hierarchical Federated Learning (HFL) has been proposed. Liu et al. introduced a Client-Edge-Cloud architecture to reduce communication latency. Briggs et al. demonstrated HFL for disparate clusters. However, these works assume trusted intermediate aggregators, whereas our Campus Aggregators act as governance boundaries.

\subsection{Handling System Heterogeneity}

FedProx adds a proximal term to tolerate device heterogeneity but does not address \textit{hierarchical} organization. Asynchronous FL allows delayed updates but lacks \textit{staleness-aware weighting}. Our staleness dampening factor $\alpha(\tau) = 1/(1+\tau)^\gamma$ bounds the influence of stale gradients while maintaining convergence.

\subsection{Cross-Silo Federated Learning}

Google's cross-silo FL targets enterprise environments (hospitals, banks) but assumes trusted aggregation servers. We introduce a \textit{campus aggregator} tier respecting institutional firewalls, enabling universities to audit local contributions before global submission.

\subsection{Differential Privacy in FL}

Abadi et al. formalized DP-SGD with gradient clipping and Gaussian noise. Geyer et al. applied DP to federated settings but only at the client level. Truex et al. proposed hybrid approaches combining DP with Multi-Party Computation (MPC). Our \textit{hierarchical DP} (edge + campus noise layers) provides stronger privacy through composition while reducing per-layer noise requirements.

\subsection{Security Threats}
Federated Learning is vulnerable to inference attacks. Shokri et al. demonstrated membership inference, while Zhu et al. showed that raw data can be reconstructed from gradients (Deep Leakage). To mitigate this, Bonawitz et al. introduced Secure Aggregation, which we implement via our hierarchical noise injection.

\subsection{Domain Adaptation & Transfer Learning}

DANN and CORAL address domain shift through adversarial or statistical alignment but require access to source and target data simultaneously—violating data minimization. Federated domain adaptation still assumes single-server topology. Our approach achieves cross-domain robustness through \textit{collaborative multi-domain training} without centralizing data.

% ==========================================
% III. HIERARCHICAL FEDAVG
% ==========================================
\section{Hierarchical Federated Averaging}

\subsection{Architectural Irreversibility Constraint}

All learning signals participating in hierarchical federated aggregation originate strictly beyond the irreversible abstraction boundary defined in the ScholarMaster canonical architecture (Paper 17). No raw biometric signals, facial images, or reconstructable identity representations are present at any tier of the federation.

Campus and global aggregation operate exclusively on task-specific, irreversibly abstracted feature representations and differentially private gradients derived from them. Consequently, cross-campus learning does not introduce new biometric exposure risks and does not weaken architectural privacy guarantees enforced at the edge.

\subsection{System Model}

Let $\mathcal{C} = \{C_1, \ldots, C_M\}$ denote $M$ campuses (institutions). Each campus $C_i$ contains $N_i$ classroom edge nodes $\{e_{i,1}, \ldots, e_{i,N_i}\}$. Edge node $e_{i,j}$ holds local dataset $\mathcal{D}_{i,j}$ with $n_{i,j}$ samples. The global dataset is partitioned as:
\[
\mathcal{D}_{\text{global}} = \bigcup_{i=1}^{M} \bigcup_{j=1}^{N_i} \mathcal{D}_{i,j}
\]

Campuses exhibit \textbf{cross-silo non-IID}: $P_{C_1}(x,y) \neq P_{C_2}(x,y)$ due to environmental and demographic differences. Edge nodes within a campus exhibit \textbf{intra-silo non-IID}: $ P_{e_{i,1}}(x,y) \neq P_{e_{i,2}}(x,y)$ due to classroom-specific variations (time of day, course type).

